A system for on-site customer feedback processing and response, the system comprising:

a feedback capture interface disposed at or accessible from a service location and configured to receive, from a customer while the customer is present at the service location, customer feedback data relating to a product, service, facility, transaction, or customer experience;

one or more processors communicatively coupled to the feedback capture interface;

a memory storing instructions which, when executed by the one or more processors, cause the system to:

receive the customer feedback data in substantially real time;

associate the customer feedback data with at least one contextual parameter, the at least one contextual parameter comprising one or more of a location identifier, a department identifier, a service provider identifier, a transaction identifier, a time stamp, a customer identifier, or a feedback category;

automatically evaluate the customer feedback data to determine at least one feedback attribute, the at least one feedback attribute comprising one or more of sentiment, urgency, severity, dissatisfaction level, issue type, escalation status, or likelihood of customer departure without resolution;

compare the at least one feedback attribute with one or more predetermined alert criteria;

select, based at least in part on the at least one feedback attribute and the at least one contextual parameter, one or more personnel designated to receive an alert;

generate, responsive to satisfaction of the one or more predetermined alert criteria, a real-time notification identifying the customer feedback data or a portion thereof and including information usable to locate, identify, or assist the customer; and

transmit the real-time notification to at least one communication device associated with the selected one or more personnel while the customer remains at the service location.

In some embodiments, the feedback capture interface comprises a kiosk, tablet computer, point-of-sale terminal, mobile application, web interface, quick-response code interface, near-field communication interface, short message service interface, voice input interface, or interactive display positioned at the service location.

In some embodiments, the automatic evaluation comprises applying natural language processing, keyword detection, sentiment analysis, rules-based scoring, machine-learning classification, or a combination thereof to textual, numeric, audio, image, or selection-based feedback data.

In some embodiments, the predetermined alert criteria comprise at least one threshold associated with a negative sentiment score, a low satisfaction rating, a complaint category, a high-priority keyword, a repeated issue, a customer status, or an operational condition at the service location.

In some embodiments, the selected personnel comprise a manager, supervisor, service representative, department employee, maintenance personnel, customer care representative, security personnel, or other staff member having authority or capability to address the issue identified in the customer feedback data.

In some embodiments, the real-time notification is transmitted by one or more of a push notification, text message, electronic mail message, voice call, pager message, wearable-device alert, dashboard update, collaboration-platform message, or point-of-sale system notification.

In some embodiments, the real-time notification includes one or more of the feedback content, severity score, recommended response action, customer location, customer name or identifier, table number, room number, queue position, transaction details, time elapsed since submission, and an escalation deadline.

In some embodiments, the instructions further cause the system to monitor whether a selected personnel member acknowledges the real-time notification within a predetermined time period and, upon failure to receive an acknowledgement, transmit an escalated notification to alternate or higher-level personnel.

In some embodiments, the instructions further cause the system to receive resolution information from the selected personnel, associate the resolution information with the customer feedback data, and store a record indicating whether the issue was addressed before the customer departed the service location.

In some embodiments, the system is configured to generate analytics based on stored feedback data and resolution information, including trends, response times, issue categories, personnel performance, location performance, or customer satisfaction improvement metrics.

A computer-implemented method for on-site customer feedback intervention comprises receiving customer feedback from a customer located at a service site, automatically analyzing the customer feedback to determine an urgency or dissatisfaction condition, selecting one or more responsible personnel according to the urgency or dissatisfaction condition and site-specific routing rules, and transmitting a real-time alert to the selected personnel to enable intervention with the customer before the customer exits the service site.

## Title  
**System and Method for Real-Time Capture of Customer Experience Feedback and Immediate Service Recovery at a Service Provider Location**

## Abstract  
An invention is disclosed for capturing customer experience feedback at or near a service provider location and enabling staff to intervene substantially immediately when a poor customer experience is reported. The system includes one or more customer feedback input devices, a feedback processing server, a rules engine configured to classify feedback as positive, neutral, or negative, and a staff notification subsystem. In response to detecting negative feedback, the system generates an actionable alert identifying the location, time, nature, and severity of the reported experience and transmits the alert to one or more staff members or managers. The system may further provide escalation, acknowledgement tracking, service recovery workflows, analytics, and follow-up communications with the customer.

## Field of the Invention  
The invention relates generally to customer experience management systems and, more particularly, to systems and methods for collecting customer feedback at a physical or service location and triggering immediate operational intervention when negative feedback is received.

## Background  
Service providers, such as restaurants, hotels, retail stores, healthcare facilities, transportation hubs, entertainment venues, and professional service offices, frequently obtain customer feedback after a customer has departed. Such feedback may be collected through email surveys, web forms, call centers, or third-party review platforms. However, delayed feedback often prevents the service provider from correcting the problem while the customer is still present or while the service failure is still capable of being remedied.

For example, a restaurant patron may experience slow service, incorrect food preparation, or dissatisfaction with cleanliness. If the restaurant learns of the complaint only after the customer leaves, the opportunity to correct the issue, preserve goodwill, or prevent a negative public review may be lost. Existing systems therefore do not adequately enable real-time service recovery based on customer feedback captured at the location.

Accordingly, there is a need for a system that captures positive or negative customer experience feedback contemporaneously with the customer’s visit and automatically notifies appropriate staff when poor feedback is reported, thereby enabling immediate intervention.

## Summary of the Invention  
The invention provides a real-time customer experience feedback and intervention system. In one embodiment, the system includes a feedback capture interface accessible to a customer at a service provider location. The feedback capture interface may be presented through a kiosk, tablet, touchscreen terminal, QR code, mobile application, near-field communication tag, payment terminal, receipt link, messaging interface, or other customer-accessible device or medium.

The customer may submit a rating, select an experience category, provide free-text comments, record audio, upload an image, or otherwise indicate whether the experience is positive or negative. The submitted feedback is transmitted to a feedback processing system, which determines whether the feedback indicates a poor experience. If the feedback meets one or more negative-feedback criteria, the system automatically generates an alert and routes the alert to designated personnel, such as a server, shift supervisor, store manager, guest relations representative, or on-duty service recovery team.

The alert may include the customer’s location within the facility, table number, room number, queue position, transaction identifier, selected complaint category, sentiment score, urgency score, timestamp, and recommended remedial action. Staff may acknowledge the alert, communicate with the customer, record remedial steps, and close the incident. If the alert is not acknowledged or resolved within a predetermined time, the system may escalate the alert to higher-level personnel.

Positive feedback may also be captured and used for employee recognition, performance analytics, customer loyalty programs, testimonial generation, or operational reporting.

## Brief Description of Example Components  
In an exemplary embodiment, the invention comprises:

1. **A customer feedback interface** configured to receive customer experience feedback at or near the service provider location.  
2. **A feedback processing engine** configured to analyze the feedback and classify the feedback as positive, neutral, or negative.  
3. **A rules and escalation engine** configured to determine whether immediate staff intervention is required.  
4. **A notification subsystem** configured to transmit real-time alerts to staff devices.  
5. **A staff response interface** configured to permit staff to acknowledge, manage, and resolve reported poor experiences.  
6. **A data storage system** configured to store feedback, response events, resolution status, and analytics.  
7. **An analytics module** configured to generate reports concerning customer satisfaction trends, response times, recurring issues, staff performance, and location-level service quality.

## Detailed Description  

### Feedback Capture  
A customer located at a service provider premises may access a feedback prompt through any suitable mechanism. For example, a QR code may be displayed on a restaurant table, hotel room card, retail receipt, fitting room sign, patient discharge document, or service counter display. Upon scanning the QR code, the customer’s mobile device presents a feedback page associated with the particular location or transaction.

In another embodiment, a touchscreen kiosk or tablet may be positioned near an exit, waiting area, dining table, reception desk, checkout counter, or service bay. The customer may select from response options such as:

- “Great experience”  
- “Everything is okay”  
- “I need assistance”  
- “I am dissatisfied”  
- “Service is too slow”  
- “My order is incorrect”  
- “The area is not clean”  
- “I would like to speak to a manager”

The interface may also request a rating, such as a one-to-five-star score, smiley-face scale, numerical satisfaction score, net promoter score, or binary positive/negative selection.

### Contextual Identification  
The system may associate submitted feedback with contextual information. Such contextual information may include, without limitation:

- Service provider location;  
- Branch, store, or venue identifier;  
- Table, room, seat, counter, department, or service station identifier;  
- Employee or team identifier;  
- Transaction number or order number;  
- Time and date;  
- Customer loyalty account or anonymous session identifier;  
- Device identifier;  
- Language preference;  
- Customer contact preference; and  
- Current operational state, such as queue length, order status, or staffing level.

This contextual information allows the service provider to identify where intervention is required without requiring the customer to repeatedly explain the problem.

### Feedback Analysis  
Upon receipt of feedback, the feedback processing engine classifies the feedback. Classification may be performed using predetermined thresholds, keyword detection, natural language processing, sentiment analysis, machine learning models, or a combination thereof.

For example, a rating of one or two stars may be classified as negative. A text comment such as “I have been waiting too long” may be classified as a service-delay complaint. A comment such as “the restroom is dirty” may be classified as a cleanliness issue. A selection of “I want help now” may be assigned a high urgency level.

The rules engine may determine whether an alert should be generated based on one or more factors, including:

- Feedback rating below a threshold;  
- Selection of a negative experience category;  
- Use of urgent or severe language;  
- Complaint from a high-value or loyalty customer;  
- Repeat complaint within a short period;  
- Complaint associated with safety, cleanliness, discrimination, billing, medical, or security concerns;  
- Lack of staff acknowledgement within a predetermined time; and  
- Aggregate negative feedback from multiple customers in the same location or time period.

### Real-Time Staff Notification  
When intervention criteria are satisfied, the system generates a real-time alert. The alert may be transmitted by push notification, SMS message, email, pager, wearable device notification, point-of-sale notification, kitchen display system message, manager dashboard update, voice assistant announcement, or other communication channel.

The alert may include concise actionable information, such as:

> “Urgent customer feedback: Table 12 reports slow service and requests assistance. Rating: 1/5. Submitted 30 seconds ago. Please acknowledge.”

In another example:

> “Negative feedback received in Room 408: cleanliness complaint. Guest requests contact. Assign housekeeping or manager.”

The notification subsystem may select recipients based on role, proximity, schedule, responsibility, department, escalation hierarchy, or workload. For example, a food quality complaint may be routed to a floor manager and kitchen lead, while a hotel room cleanliness complaint may be routed to housekeeping and the duty manager.

### Staff Response and Intervention  
A staff member receiving the alert may access a staff response interface on a mobile device, workstation, wearable device, or management console. The staff response interface may permit the staff member to:

- Acknowledge receipt of the alert;  
- Accept responsibility for the intervention;  
- View feedback details;  
- Contact the customer by text, phone, in-app message, or in-person visit;  
- Assign the issue to another staff member;  
- Select a recovery action;  
- Record notes;  
- Mark the issue as resolved; and  
- Request follow-up feedback.

Examples of service recovery actions include offering a replacement item, discount, apology, expedited service, room change, housekeeping visit, manager visit, loyalty points, refund, or other remedy.

### Escalation  
If no staff member acknowledges the alert within a first predetermined period, the system may send a reminder or route the alert to additional personnel. If the issue is not resolved within a second predetermined period, the system may escalate the matter to a general manager, regional manager, corporate customer care team, or other designated escalation recipient.

Escalation rules may vary according to severity. For example, a low rating with no comment may require acknowledgement within five minutes, while a safety-related complaint may require acknowledgement within thirty seconds and escalation after one minute.

### Positive Feedback Handling  
The system is not limited to negative feedback. Positive feedback may be captured and processed for operational use. For example, when a customer submits a high rating or compliment, the system may:

- Notify the relevant employee or manager;  
- Store the compliment in an employee recognition database;  
- Invite the customer to post a public review;  
- Trigger a loyalty reward;  
- Generate performance metrics; or  
- Identify best practices associated with high satisfaction.

### Analytics and Reporting  
The data storage and analytics subsystem may aggregate feedback and staff response data. The system may generate dashboards showing:

- Volume of positive and negative feedback;  
- Common complaint categories;  
- Average response time;  
- Average resolution time;  
- Escalation frequency;  
- Staff acknowledgement rates;  
- Satisfaction before and after intervention;  
- Location-by-location comparison;  
- Department-level performance; and  
- Correlation between feedback and operational variables.

Such analytics may be used to improve staffing, training, inventory, scheduling, facility maintenance, and service procedures.

## Example Use Case  
A customer dining at a restaurant scans a QR code displayed on the table. The customer selects “Poor experience” and enters “We have been waiting 25 minutes and no one has taken our order.” The system automatically associates the feedback with Table 14 and the restaurant location. Because the rating is negative and the category is service delay, the system generates an urgent alert to the floor manager’s mobile device.

The floor manager receives the alert, acknowledges it, and visits Table 14 within two minutes. The manager apologizes, assigns a server, and offers a complimentary appetizer. The manager records the action in the staff response interface and marks the incident resolved. The system later sends the customer a short follow-up prompt asking whether the issue was satisfactorily addressed.

## Advantages  
The invention provides several advantages over conventional feedback systems, including:

- Capturing customer dissatisfaction while the customer is still reachable;  
- Enabling immediate service recovery;  
- Reducing negative public reviews;  
- Improving customer retention;  
- Providing accountability for staff response;  
- Identifying recurring operational problems;  
- Recognizing positive staff performance; and  
- Creating measurable feedback-to-resolution workflows.

## Representative Claim-Style Description  
A customer experience intervention system may comprise a customer feedback interface configured to receive customer experience feedback from a customer at a service provider location; a processor configured to classify the feedback as indicating a positive experience or a negative experience; a rules engine configured to determine, based at least in part on the classification, whether staff intervention is required; and a notification subsystem configured to transmit, in response to determining that staff intervention is required, an alert to at least one staff device associated with the service provider location, wherein the alert enables a staff member to intervene with the customer substantially in real time.

In some embodiments, the system further comprises an acknowledgement module configured to receive an acknowledgement from the staff member, an escalation module configured to transmit the alert to a second staff member if acknowledgement is not received within a predetermined time, and a resolution module configured to store one or more remedial actions taken by the staff member.
